  "result": "Excellent! All 136 tests pass. Here's a summary of the date utilities implementation:\n\n**Changes made:**\n\n1. **New src/dates.js module** with three UTC-based date functions:\n   - `isValidDate(dateStr)`: Validates YYYY-MM-DD format and real dates (handles leap years correctly)\n   - `addDays(dateStr, n)`: Adds n days to a date, returns YYYY-MM-DD format\n   - `daysBetween(dateA, dateB)`: Calculates whole days between two dates (b minus a)\n\n2. **All operations use UTC** (`T00:00:00Z`):\n   - Uses `new Date(dateStr + 'T00:00:00Z')` to parse dates in UTC\n   - Uses `setUTCDate()` and `toISOString()` for calculations\n   - Never uses local time zone conversions\n\n3. **Updated createInvoice**:\n   - Removed local `isValidDate()` function\n   - Now imports and uses `isValidDate` from dates.js\n   - Ensures consistent date validation across the codebase\n\n4. **Comprehensive test coverage (21 date tests, all passing)**:\n   - isValidDate validation (format, impossible dates, leap years)\n   - addDays (month/year boundaries, negative offsets, large offsets)\n   - daysBetween (positive/negative differences, year boundaries, large gaps)\n   - Inverse relationship tests (addDays/daysBetween)\n   - UTC operation verification\n   - Leap year handling (2024 is leap, 2023 is not)\n\n**Key design decisions:**\n- All dates are YYYY-MM-DD strings everywhere\n- All date arithmetic is strictly UTC-based\n- The T00:00:00Z suffix ensures consistent UTC interpretation\n- Leap year logic is handled correctly by JavaScript's Date object",
